Here's what I'd gather before designing anything, grouped by area:

**1. Process & functional requirements**
- What the equipment does, step by step (process description, P&IDs, sequence of operations)
- Operating modes: auto, manual, semi-auto, maintenance, recipe/batch
- Throughput, cycle time, accuracy and repeatability targets
- Start-up, shutdown, and changeover behavior

**2. Safety (ask this early)**
- Hazard/risk assessment and required safety level (SIL/PL, Category)
- Applicable standards: ISO 13849, IEC 62061, IEC 61508, NFPA 79, IEC 60204-1, OSHA
- E-stops, guards, light curtains, interlocks, safe torque off
- Fail-safe states and what must happen on power loss or air loss

**3. I/O and field devices**
- Complete I/O list: digital/analog in/out, signal types (4-20 mA, 24 VDC, PT100, etc.)
- Sensors, actuators, drives, valves, motors, instruments, with make/model preferences
- Motion needs: servo, VFD, positioning, synchronization
- Hazardous areas (ATEX/Class I Div 2) and environment (IP rating, temperature, washdown)

**4. Control hardware & software**
- Customer or site standard for PLC/DCS/PAC brand (Siemens, Rockwell, etc.)
- Programming language (IEC 61131-3) and code standards/libraries
- Spare capacity (typically 20% I/O), memory, scan time requirements
- Redundancy needs (CPU, power, network)

**5. Communication & integration**
- Fieldbus/network: PROFINET, EtherNet/IP, Modbus, IO-Link, OPC UA
- Integration with SCADA/MES/ERP/historian
- Remote access and cybersecurity requirements (IEC 62443)

**6. HMI/SCADA & operator needs**
- Screens, alarms, trends, user roles and access levels
- Alarm philosophy (ISA-18.2), event logging, audit trails
- Language and operator skill level

**7. Data & compliance**
- Data logging, traceability, reporting
- Regulatory requirements (FDA 21 CFR Part 11, GMP, CE, UL)

**8. Electrical & panel design**
- Power supply, voltage, grounding, short-circuit ratings
- Panel space, cooling, cable routing, field wiring standards
- Drawings (schematics, layouts, BOM)

**9. Project & commercial**
- Scope boundaries: who supplies hardware, mechanical, commissioning?
- Budget, schedule, and delivery milestones
- Existing equipment or legacy systems to integrate with

**10. Testing & handover**
- FAT/SAT criteria and acceptance tests
- Simulation or digital twin options before commissioning
- Documentation: manuals, as-built drawings, backups, training
- Spares, maintenance strategy, and long-term support/obsolescence

**Key questions I'd always ask the client:**
1. What happens when something fails? (Safe state, recovery)
2. Is this a new build or retrofit?
3. Who will maintain it, and what's their skill level?
4. Are there any standards or preferred vendors I must follow?